                "content": "The textarea element in React is slightly different from ordinary HTML.\n\nIn HTML the value of a textarea is the text between the start tag \n<textarea> \nand the end tag </textarea>.\n\n <textarea>\n Content of the textarea.\n</textarea>\n\nIn React the value of a textarea is placed in a value attribute, just like with the input element.\n\nWe'll use the useState Hook to manage the value of the textarea:\n\nExample:\nReact uses the value attribute to control the textarea:\n\nimport { createRoot } from 'react-dom/client'\nimport { useState } from 'react'\n\nfunction MyForm() {\n const [mytxt, setMytxt] = useState(\"\");\n\n function handleChange(e) {\n setMytxt(e.target.value);\n }\n\n return (\n <form>\n <label>Write here:\n <textarea\n value={mytxt}\n onChange={handleChange}\n />\n </label>\n <p>Current value: {mytxt}</p>\n </form>\n )\n}\n\ncreateRoot(document.getElementById('root')).render(\n <MyForm />\n);\n\nRun \nExample »",
